From fa7c8872f797f1b2844d9de00a4aea4c43eee317 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 22:11:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07588/11362] [outer-loop] REQ-ARC-WMTE-5828: larger-budget
 re-run + wire into offline dev-twin live path

Operator directive: "run that larger budget test. also, why did we only
wire that into offline and not live agent?"

Larger-budget result (max_nodes=300 vs 15, depth_cap=40 vs 15, seed
58280): still levels_gained=0 on sc25/lf52/bp35, but no longer
uniformly shallow. sc25 (states_expanded=95/300) CONVERGED -- exhausted
its own search frontier under the dead-end pruner rather than being
budget-capped, a materially stronger negative than "budget
inconclusive" for that game specifically. bp35 (302/300) used the full
budget without converging -- still genuinely budget-bound. lf52
(states_expanded=3) was anomalously shallower than the first run's 16
-- reported honestly, flagged as an unresolved anomaly, not explained
away. Recalibration found while preparing this run: ops/arc_solve_
registry.yaml shows all three games are full_game_clear=true but only
via hand-crafted per-game GameAdapters (e.g. lf52's registry cites a
146-action L3 sequence, a 927-action L8+ probe) -- even 20x the
original budget is not provably sufficient for these games' actual
difficulty.

Live-path wiring gap: arc_tool_loop_lookahead.py was reachable from
NEITHER canonical live entrypoint, not even the offline dev-twin
(scripts/arc_loop_solve.py) -- only from its own standalone A/B
script. arc_orphan_solver_lint.py's closure check confirmed this and
didn't catch it because the module's shape doesn't match the lint's
solver-like heuristic. Fixed: added solve_via_tool_loop_lookahead() to
arc_loop_solve.py, dispatched via --mechanism tool_loop_lookahead
(alternative to the existing graph_explore default) plus
--ignore-adapter for A/B comparison against a hand-authored adapter.
Real end-to-end smoke-tested; lint now passes clean. Wiring into the
SCORED E3AgentPolicy cascade deliberately deferred -- the mechanism is
still unproven and costs up to 12 real LLM calls per decision, so
adding it to the actual scored path now would risk hurting live
efficiency for no demonstrated correctness benefit.

